      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "An audit of opioid prescribing in a large teaching hospital across all specialties was conducted to identify areas for improvement."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Opioid medications prescribed for the entire patient admission were recorded and assessed using quality statements."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "RESULTS AND DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Of 334 patients reviewed 209 (62.6%) were prescribed an opioid. Poly-prescribing of 'when required' (p.r.n.) opioids was frequent with 107 (51.2%) patients having more than one 'p.r.n.' opioid. Dosing intervals were too long for 146 (69.8%) patients leaving them at risk of breakthrough pain. The intramuscular route was prescribed for 100 (47.8%) patients, and 49 (23.4%) of prescriptions had an inappropriate variety of administration routes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Although the criteria for assessing opioid prescribing were stringent the prescribing was sub-optimal. This survey will form the basis for future quality initiatives."
        }
      ]},
